# The TLS terminator, for a shop that puts its server anywhere but its own
# desk.
#
# What crosses the wire between a till and the shop is a bearer credential and
# the day's sales. On a shop's wifi, which has one password that the delivery
# man also knows, in the clear, that is somebody else's shop's takings for the
# price of sitting outside it.
#
# Two ways to get a certificate, and the difference matters more than the
# configuration does:
#
#   OPENPOS_HOST=shop.example.com   a name that resolves to this machine, with
#                                   80 and 443 reachable from the internet.
#                                   Caddy gets a real certificate on its own and
#                                   renews it, and every tablet trusts it with
#                                   no work at all.
#
#   OPENPOS_HOST=till.local         anything else. Caddy makes its own authority
#                                   and signs for that name, which no tablet
#                                   trusts until somebody installs the authority
#                                   on each one. That is a real afternoon, and it
#                                   is the honest price of a shop with no domain.
#
# The internal case is the default here because it works on a bench with no
# domain and no open ports, which is where anybody reads this first.

{
	# Where Caddy writes what it has been told about certificates. Named so the
	# volume in compose has an obvious reason to exist: without it, a restart
	# asks a certificate authority for another one, and authorities have limits.
	storage file_system /data
}

{$OPENPOS_HOST:till.local} {
	# `tls internal` for a name nobody can prove, which Caddy signs with an
	# authority of its own. Delete this line for a real domain and Caddy will
	# fetch a real certificate instead: it is the presence of the line, not the
	# name, that decides.
	tls {$OPENPOS_TLS:internal}

	# One hop, and it must match OPENPOS_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS on the server.
	# Behind a proxy every request arrives from the proxy's address, so without
	# this the whole shop shares one rate-limit bucket and one guessed
	# enrolment code locks out every tablet in the building.
	reverse_proxy api:8080 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
	}
}
